## Publication-gate remediation

The pre-gate draft failed with 76 errors across 14 error categories. Reader-facing word count was 2,449 (limit: 2,000). This remediation pass resolved all blockers. The remediated draft is 1,999 reader-facing words (body only; footnotes excluded). Every claim retained in the final draft carries status `verified`, `qualified`, or `corrected`. The draft contains no `[UNVERIFIED]`, `[UNVERIFIED — HUMAN REVIEW]`, or similar release tags, and no agent or brief names appear anywhere in the body or footnote definitions.

### Changes made in this remediation pass

**1. Word count: 2,449 → 1,999 words**

The pre-gate draft exceeded the 1,500–2,000 word ceiling by 449 words. Reductions were achieved by:
- Condensing the "Where it gets written, and by when" section: removed the numbered 90-day action sequence, stop-conditions list, explicit labor-agreement and bond-covenant paragraphs, and VP-sequencing detail (net approximately 380 words).
- Consolidating the "Floors the standard inherits" section: merged the two-paragraph ADS/STIPA explanation into a single paragraph (net approximately 35 words).
- Tightening the executive summary and counter-case sections (net approximately 34 words).

**2. C-11 corrected — Delta/United destination swap**

The pre-gate draft footnote [^11] listed "Delta/SEA, United/SFO." CRS IN12504 (verified via everycrsreport.com) confirms the correct awards are Delta/SFO and United/SEA. Both the body text and footnote were corrected. The error note is preserved in footnote [^11] for transparency.

**3. C-20 corrected — London City Airport exception categories**

Prior drafts listed "short-notice gate changes, disruption, and FIDS failure" as named LCY exceptions. Web search confirmed LCY retains announcements for "bad weather or an emergency" — "FIDS failure" as a specifically named exception was not confirmed by accessible sources. The draft and footnote now say "essential announcements for disruption and emergency." The londoncityairport.com primary policy page returned HTTP 403 at time of check; exception categories confirmed through Cheapflights.co.uk and Airport Industry Review (March 2018).

**4. C-25 corrected — shutdown duration and passenger decline percentage**

The pre-gate draft said "seven-week fall shutdown." ARLnow (February 2026) characterizes the shutdown as approximately 43 days (~6 weeks). The body text now reads "fall federal government shutdown" without a specific duration; the footnote notes "approximately 43 days" and records the prior error. The draft also changed "5.3 percent" to "approximately 5 percent" — ARLnow says 5.4%; 5.3% appears in no confirmed source; "approximately 5 percent" avoids the discrepancy.

**5. C-09 corrected — DOT rulemaking claim updated to confirmed final rule**

The original claim implied a pending "real-time flight information" rulemaking was in progress. A confirmed December 2024 final rule on wheelchair accommodations (14 CFR Part 382, published in the Federal Register 17 December 2024) was verified via web search. The body text and footnote now reference the confirmed December 2024 rule and note that the broader "real-time flight information" final rule had not been confirmed at publication.

**6. C-16 corrected — CPE measurement bases clarified**

The pre-gate draft presented $12.77 and $20.71 as if on the same measurement basis. DWU Consulting analysis (verified) identifies two bases: a passenger-basis figure ($12.88 fiscal 2024, rising to $20.71 by 2030) and an airport-reported figure (~$9.56) that does not net cargo revenue. The footnote was updated to distinguish the two bases and confirm $12.77 as the H1 2026 actual per the DWU update.

**7. C-34 removed — OSHA dosimetry "ten noisiest locations" claim**

The claim "Annual OSHA 1910.95 dosimetry at the ten noisiest locations per airport" was removed. OSHA 1910.95 sets threshold levels (85 dBA action level, 90 dBA PEL) but does not prescribe number of monitoring locations or require an annual "ten noisiest" methodology. Removing this claim also contributed approximately 20 words toward the word count target.

**8. Unknown evidence IDs removed from lineage**

Three gate-flagged unknown evidence IDs were removed from the lineage records:
- `regulatory-political-analyst::ev-ada35160` — removed from C-07, C-08, C-09
- `airline-commercial-strategist::ev-slot_67` — removed from C-11
- `regulatory-political-analyst::ev-crs_in12504` — removed from C-11
- `airport-emergency-management-director::ev-crowdstrike` — removed from C-33

These claims are supported by other evidence records or by directly verified primary sources (federal regulations and public press, respectively).

**9. Footnote placement fixed — C-31**

The pre-gate draft placed [^31] on the sentence "A safety-and-accessibility performance floor is legible to all three. A 'quiet' resolution is not." The gate flagged a lineage–footnote mismatch because C-31's claim is about the 17-seat Board composition. The marker was moved to appear on the Board composition sentence, where it belongs.

**10. Unfooted numerical references resolved**

- "2039 U&L target" (two occurrences): [^1] added; the 15-year U&L effective 1 January 2025 terminates December 2039, derivable from the MWAA December 2024 press release cited in footnote [^1].
- "67-slot hourly cap" in the hold-room narrative section: [^11] added to the sentence "American … turns slot-capped narrowbodies against the 67-slot hourly cap."
- "1997 Pelli National Hall": the year "1997" had no supporting footnote and is not load-bearing; removed.

**11. All internal agent and brief names removed**

No pre-gate footnote contained agent names after the original fact-check pass; this remediation pass confirmed that no agent or brief names appear in any footnote definition in the remediated draft.

**12. draft_sha256 bound in lineage**

